# Generated pages (`boss playbook`, `design`, `board --html`, `help --html`, and the home
# that lists them) — pure projections of your docs/, re-rendered on
# demand. Committing them means a long diff every time one moves, for files
# nobody reads from git.
.boss/board.html
.boss/playbook.html
.boss/design.html
.boss/help.html
.boss/index.html

# Copies BOSS keeps of files it was about to overwrite (`boss sync`). Local recovery, not
# project history — and the reason an overwrite is never the end of the story.
.boss/backups/

# Per-person conscience state — stays local, never shared with a cofounder (DEC-001).
# Everything else BOSS writes (docs/ ideas, canvas, decisions, RESUME, and the venture
# brain's read.md) DOES commit, except sensitive research (below) — so pushing backs up your
# thinking AND keeps your cofounder in the loop. But the conscience's relationship with YOU — what it said and
# what you did with it — is yours alone; it never travels to a teammate.
#
# DEC-015 moved the live copies OUT of the project entirely, to ~/.boss/projects/<key>/,
# because "gitignored in this directory" stopped meaning "private to this person" the
# moment a session could be spawned in its own git worktree — the same person, a
# different directory, and a conscience that had forgotten every nudge it ever fired.
# These rules are still here on purpose: a project created before that move keeps its
# old copies on disk (migration copies, never deletes), and they must stay ignored.
.boss/brain/relationship.md
.boss/trace.jsonl

# Research that is about people, or isn't yours to publish — stays on this machine. Interview
# notes are other people's words, quoted; what you import is raw material you haven't sorted yet;
# notes on rivals are intelligence. A repo can go public in one click, and its history goes with it.
# Research about no one — API notes, build-vs-buy, public market figures — commits, so your agents
# and a teammate can use it. To share these with a teammate: `boss team share <shared folder>`.
# No trailing slash, on purpose — `boss team share` turns each into a link, and `docs/evidence/`
# would match only a real folder, so the link would be committed.
docs/evidence
docs/source
docs/competition
